# Latency SCALE corpus — the deterministic drive behind `just latency-scale-gate`.
#
# Same JSONL schema and same harness as keystone.jsonl and latency-ratchet.jsonl
# (see docs/Testing/HandAuthoredRegressions.md). This sidecar exists for the one
# axis the other two cannot express: CORPUS SIZE.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE CORPUS FROM latency-ratchet.jsonl
# The ratchet corpus cycles three documents, so after the first three
# navigations every switch re-uses a cached materialized view. Navigation's
# scale cost is paid on the FIRST visit to a block — the engine mints one
# materialized view per watched block, and its recursive descendant walk costs
# O(vault). A rung built from repeat visits is structurally blind to it. Every
# navigation below therefore goes to a block never visited before.
#
# WHY EACH NAVIGATION IS TWO TRANSITIONS
# `settle_focus_matviews` (inside NavigateFocus) polls only the lightweight
# `current_focus` / `focus_roots` tables, and the headless harness never
# registers the main panel's focus-descendant content watch. A bare
# NavigateFocus is therefore blind to the cold materialization that dominates a
# real navigation. `SetupWatch` registers exactly the watch the real main panel
# registers, through the same production `ReactiveEngine::watch_query_live`
# path, so the pair adds up to one user-visible navigation.
#
# WHY THE COUNT IS 32
# `measure_latency.py` derives percentiles by interpolation, and the gate
# demands >= 30 samples per rung so no statistic rests on a handful of points
# (see the latency-ratchet.jsonl header for the measurement that forced this).
# Thirty-two navigations give 32 samples on each of the two rungs. The seed must
# supply at least 32 soak pages or the replay fails loud on a missing block.
